# RAG Vector Store

The ticket database is embedded H2. The vector index is Spring AI `SimpleVectorStore` (in-memory, optional JSON `save`/`load`). Embeddings come from Ollama running in Docker. H2 and embedded PostgreSQL cannot run pgvector; do not add pgvector unless the architecture decision changes.

## Ingestion

- Ingest only approved knowledge-base documents and ticket content the product decision says is searchable.
- Ingestion is idempotent. Running it twice must not duplicate chunks.
- Do not call Ollama inside a database transaction.
- On startup, either re-ingest from the source documents or `load` a previously saved store. Document which one the runtime uses.

## Chunking

- Chunk by structure first (headings, paragraphs), then by size.
- Keep chunks small enough for the embedding model context and large enough to hold one support fact. Record the target size and overlap in configuration, not in code literals.
- Do not split a ticket id, status, or title away from the sentence that states it.

## Metadata

Every chunk stores:

- `sourceId` — stable document or ticket id
- `sourceType` — `ticket` or `knowledge-base`
- `chunkIndex` — order within the source
- `contentHash` — hash of the source text used to detect staleness
- `title` — human-readable label for citations

Do not store secrets or raw credentials in chunk text or metadata.

## Embedding model

- Use the Ollama embedding model named in configuration (the current candidate is `nomic-embed-text`).
- The vector dimension must match that model. Changing model or dimension requires a full re-index.
- One embedding model is authoritative at a time. Do not mix vectors from two models in one store.

## Vector store

- Build `SimpleVectorStore` as an explicit `@Bean`. It has no starter auto-configuration.
- This store is for development and evaluation. It scans all vectors. Do not treat it as a production index.
- If the store is persisted, the JSON file lives under a configured path and is gitignored.

## top-K and threshold

- `topK` and the minimum similarity score come from configuration and can change without a code edit.
- Retrieval returns at most `topK` chunks whose score is at or above the threshold.
- A chunk below the threshold is not context, even if it is the best hit.

## Re-ingestion and stale documents

- When source text changes, `contentHash` changes. Delete that `sourceId`'s chunks before inserting the new ones.
- When a source is removed, delete its chunks. Do not leave them searchable.
- A full rebuild must replace the store contents so retired documents cannot be retrieved.

## Retrieval quality

- Log source id and score for each returned chunk at debug level. Do not log full chunk text at info.
- A retrieval change ships with an evaluation update (see the testing rule): at least one expected hit, one expected miss, and one below-threshold case.
- Prefer fewer relevant chunks over filling `topK` with weak matches.
